## Caveat: this denominator rests on a CFR assumption
The 20,960 denominator applies a 12.5% CFR to the StatsSA MACOD death count — but that CFR is from NMC-*notified* severe cases (the sicker end). The undetected majority are likely milder; a blended CFR of 3–5% implies 52,000–87,000 true cases and a status-quo P_detect of 1–2%. Relative option rankings (S1, S2, MVP) are stable across this range; absolute gap size is not. Mid-burden is used as the working denominator throughout.
:::

### Detection vs. true burden — a critical distinction

Better surveillance has **two opposite numerical effects** that decision-makers must not confuse:

| Effect | Direction | Driver | Interpretation |
|:-------|:---------:|:-------|:---------------|
| **Observed** burden | ↑ | Better case-finding (auto-notify, MVD ingest) | The headline "case count" will rise as detection improves — this is success, not failure. |
| **True** burden | ↓ | Faster source removal, targeted regulation, prevention | Number of actual poisonings falls as cases are detected and acted on earlier. |

Post-pilot case counts will rise — this is a detection effect, not a true burden increase. Interpret against completeness-adjusted true incidence (laboratory + PIH denominators as proxy); agree this framing with NDoH and MAC before pilot launch.

