## 5. Policy options

::: {.callout-tip icon=false}
## ✓ NHLS–NMC IT feed — now operational

The NHLS–NICD LIS-to-NMC connector is operational; monthly BChE data flows to NICD under the existing MOU. The three active options below build on this.
:::

### Option S1 — PIH live dashboard + Minimum Viable Dataset *(★ recommended)*

Two layers:

- **Short term (1–2 weeks):** PIH publishes a live dashboard of pesticide-related calls (counts, geography, agent, severity, demographics, **AfriTox agent name**). No new data; just a daily extract from existing call records.
- **Medium term (3–9 months):** PIH and NICD agree a **Minimum Viable Dataset (MVD)** so the ~1,158 clinician-initiated calls/year can be ingested into NMC as a structured surveillance stream. **Agent-name capture is a mandatory MVD field** — both PIH and NMC must record the AfriTox product name for every case. To make this reciprocal, **NMC should add a structured agent drop-down** (pesticide class → active ingredient → AfriTox product name) to its NDoH-hosted notification form: PIH records what the caller *reports*; NMC records what the clinician *confirms*. Combined: agent-attribution signal system-wide at near-zero marginal cost.

Detection rises to ~10 % of true cases (up from ~5 %) for **R82,000/year** (includes AfriTox agent-name harmonization). The dashboard is the lowest-hanging fruit in the whole portfolio: it ships in days.

### Option S1b — Phase 2 community layer *(commission after MVP pilot if rural/informal gap confirms)*

S1 and S2 improve detection within the **formal healthcare system** — via PIH, NHLS BChE, and NMC-notified facilities. They structurally cannot reach cases that never arrive at a formal facility: the child taken to a traditional healer after ingesting street-sold granules, the farm worker who doesn't present at all, the informal-settlement cluster whose care pathway bypasses formal services entirely.

**The MVP ceiling (~19% P_detect) is real and bounded** by the reach of formal administrative streams. S1b is the mechanism for going beyond it — but only after the MVP has established the baseline and confirmed that the remaining gap is structural rather than simply a compliance issue within the formal system.

**Trigger condition.** Commission S1b if, after 6 months of MVP operation, the geographic distribution of PIH + NHLS + NMC cases shows ≥40% of severe cases originating in districts with low formal-facility access (defined as <1 public hospital per 50km radius). This is a data-driven go/no-go, not a discretionary expansion. CSV parameters: `d_S1b_event_community_*`, `c_S1b_setup_*`, `c_S1b_op_*`, `p_fp_S1b_*`.

**False positives are a design parameter, not a disqualifier.** The gross false-positive rate is ~35% (range 20–50%). At P_detect = 0.40 on the mid-burden of 20,960 true cases, the gross notification volume would be ~12,898 events/yr — of which ~8,384 are true cases and ~4,514 are false alerts *before filtering*. This is solvable with a **two-tier triage architecture** — the same design used by WHO-EWARN, APSED, and comparable LMIC systems:

1. **Tier 1 — CHW structured checklist**: Community health workers report via a mobile checklist requiring ≥2 of: acute onset, ≥2 affected persons, known or suspected product contact. Events not meeting criteria are logged but no dispatch is triggered.
2. **Tier 2 — EHP call-back within 24 hours**: A provincial EHP calls back to confirm before committing to on-site investigation.

With a two-tier filter, the effective net FP rate drops to approximately 8–12% — within the investigation capacity modelled in the [Coordination brief](coordination_policy.qmd).

**Two-stage rollout — Free State and Gauteng first.** These two provinces account for an estimated **~50% of the annual pesticide-poisoning burden** (working assumption; to be confirmed against the MVP pilot's geographic distribution from NMC, NHLS, and PIH data). Comparable LMIC systems have achieved P_detect in the range 0.25–0.55 — 1.3–2.9× the MVP ceiling.

| Stage | Provinces | Est. burden share | Setup cost | Operating cost/yr | Go/no-go criterion |
|:------|:----------|------------------:|-----------:|------------------:|:-------------------|
| **S1b pilot** | Free State + Gauteng | ~50% | ~R1M | ~R0.8M/yr | Month 12: net P_detect >35% and net FP <15% |
| **S1b national** | All 9 provinces | 100% | ~R3.5M | ~R2.5M/yr | Conditional on pilot outcome |

The cost range (R1M–R3.5M setup; R0.8M–R2.5M/yr operating — approximately 6–18× the MVP operating cost) directly reflects the two-stage choice.

### Option S2 — BChE auto-notification *(★ recommended — capability exists)*

Configure the NHLS LIS to fire an automatic NICD notification when a BChE result shows severe inhibition (< 50 % of normal), within 24 hours of validation. **No new test, no new reagent contract** — only an IT configuration on top of the ~10,626 BChE tests/year already performed (1,779 severe in 2023). Detection rises to ~13 % of true cases (up from ~5 %) for **R55,000/year** in 2–4 weeks. This is the highest-value, fastest option on the menu.

### Option S3 — Sentinel live-patient toxicology *(★ recommended, medium term — distinct purpose)*

NHLS currently performs chemical toxicology (qualitative/quantitative pesticide assays) **post-mortem only**. There is no live-patient toxicology capability. Option S3 funds a sentinel tertiary site (e.g. CHB) to perform live-patient chemical toxicology, scaling to 4–5 sites in phase 2–3.

::: {.callout-caution appearance="minimal"}
**Forensic chemistry is fully de-identified and not fit for surveillance linkage.** No patient, facility, or case-linkage identifiers: cannot be matched to NMC or PIH records without structural re-design of specimen accessioning. The **MLDI system** is the longer-term pathway for surveillance-ready post-mortem toxicology — a separate workstream from S3.
:::

**S3 has a different purpose from S1 and S2** and is *not* a detection-coverage uplift. S1 and S2 increase the share of true cases that enter the surveillance system. **S3 identifies the proportion of chemical agents being found in severely poisoned living patients at sentinel sites** — the missing evidence base for product-specific regulation (see [Terbufos brief](terbufos_policy.qmd)). Without S3, ~10,000 survivors/year are recorded with agent "unknown / suspected" and targeted product restriction has no quantitative footing.

Cost: ~R1.2M pilot site → ~R3.6M for 5-site rollout, 6–12 months. Funded from research-surveillance streams (SAMRC, Bloomberg, Gates), not the surveillance operating budget. Full quantitative concept in the [sentinel toxicology appendix](surveillance_concept_note.qmd).

